a victim of domestic violence and the  grandparents of a disabled teenager have  won a court of appeal challenge against  the so called bedroom tax it was argued  in both cases that the policy was  discriminatory the government they are  to appeal against the decision in March  on joy now by the teenager's grandfather  Paul Rutherford who looks after his  fifteen year old grandson Warren very  good afternoon to you explain why you  went to court over this well we just  thought that the whole thing was  massively unfair on us and well a lot of  other people as well so we decided that  we have to stand up and challenge it how  did this wasn't an easy decision but how  did the so-called bedroom tax affect you  and your circumstances can you hear me  I'll try again  I was asking or how does that bedroom  taxes affect you in your circumstances  you look after fifteen-year-old Warren  who is disabled and I understand you  have a another bedroom a third bedroom  so that an overnight carer can look  after him just explain how it works  well we have we have carers who come in  every day anyway to look after Warren  with us all for us and he needs looking  after 24/7 so when we get tired and you  know if nobody can look after anybody  for all that length of time not with the  level of needs that Warren has and we  need a break we get tired I'm not well  and you know when when we have the  opportunity to have the carers to come  and stay and take that strain for us for  a night a week then that's what we would  do and we don't understand why it's such  a big deal that we we're seen to be  under occupying a bungalow that was  essentially well in fact was built for  Warren in the first place well you told  you would have to give up that bedroom  or risk losing benefits what were you  told by the council  we were we were told that we were going  to have our benefits cut because the  Housing Benefit cut because the bedroom  tax had come in and we were going to  lose 14% of our ends a week and we  couldn't understand why we just didn't  understand it and we would just I don't  know we were we were in shock for a  while and eventually our story broke in  the in the national press and things  escalated and we found ourselves being  assisted to take the government to court  to try and get the law changed now  councils do have provisioned only to  make exceptions in some cases would they  not do that for you well they did that  they refused our applications three  times and then it was it wasn't till  after our car story had been on the  national news that we got the  discretionary payment but the  discretionary payments isn't a guarantee  there's no there's no security in it  there's no sort of letter that says you  will get this money next year you know  we don't know the government could stop  giving that money to the council's next  year for all we know it's not it's not a  secure position to be in not when we've  got a child like Warren whose whole life  is depending on on how we are able to  look after him and it's enough of a  strain as it is without the added stress  of being unsure we're not feeling secure  in what was our own home just tell us  what kind of a difference it does make  to your lives to Warren's life having  that extra bit of help  it's a massive difference because  without that without the help I don't  think we could actually look after him  because he does need looking after all  the time if if he chooses he won't go to  sleep tonight there's nothing we can do  to make him oh he can be awake all night  all day  we have to carry on with all the  everyday jobs like shopping and cooking  and cleaning washing and still look  after Warren and in the end you just  can't sustain that level of care on your  own and it's hard to ask for help but we  have to and we've been provided with the  help to care for Warren and we couldn't  understand still don't why the  government can't see that and just give  us the help that we need it's not like  we don't think we're asking for very  much there's a lot of people out there  as well who've had who are in our  position who've been told that they  can't have a discretionary housing  payment and that is people who are in  exactly the same circumstances as  ourselves and for that reason if nothing  else all people in our situation should  be exempted from the bedroom tax rather  than rely on discretionary payments  which the word itself says it all is  discretionary so it's out of our hands  we just want the peace to know that we  can look after Warren without the stress  of no of worrying about whether or not  we'll be able to stay in the house we'll  have to move whether he'll have to go  into care him going into care is  something that our lives are dedicated  to preventing and we don't want we will  fight anybody who makes that look like  it might have to happen including as  we've done now we'll fight the  government it doesn't matter it's Warren  who's the priority and all the other  disabled kids disabled people and  anybody else who's affected by this  policy you have won today Paul but the  government says they're going to appeal  against it what would you say to  ministers who are listening I'd say they  have no idea what they're doing and I  would say that they don't they should  look closely they should look at Warren  they should look at myself and they  should ask themselves what they are  doing what do you hope to achieve by  this by  you know what is the ultimate aim to  grind us down even further is that what  you want to achieve do you want Warren  to go into care do you want me to have a  nervous breakdown or my wife who you  know it's like what do you want or do  you want us the citizens of your country  to be able to just live our lives and  give this man young man the best that we  can that's what we want to do and it's  over to them what they want to do now  Paul Rutherford thank you for your time  and also the Lawrence for joining us  today no I think you've summed it up  thank you very much
 

